    description: "Goplus, a Costway brand, sells a wide range of low-cost home fitness equipment, and this is its plain under-desk walking pad. The listing gives a brushless 2.5 HP motor rated below 45 dB, a 0.6 to 4 mph speed range split into working, walking and jogging bands, three countdown programs (time from 5 to 60 minutes, distance and calories), a seven-layer diamond-textured belt, a 4.7-inch slim body with two wheels and no assembly. Control is the included remote plus an LED display showing speed, distance, time and calories. There is no app in the listing, which some people will see as a feature: nothing to download, no account and nothing to break after an update. The cost of that simplicity is that the listing names no backup if the remote is lost. Goplus does sell its own replacement remote for its older Superfit treadmills, an infrared unit at about $33, but this pad is not on its compatibility list, so ask Goplus support for the matching part. The bullets also do not state a maximum user weight; check the listing’s specification table or ask the seller. Amazon showed it at $149.99 on 24 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "fousae-10-incline-walking-pad",
    name: "FOUSAE Walking Pad with 10% Incline",
    brand: "FOUSAE",
    category: "Highest weight limit under $150",
    badge: "Best for Heavier Users",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HPP73HXT?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410kTfnlzkL._SL500_.jpg","width":472,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $142 (Amazon, 24 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":142,"high":142},
    specs: "40 x 16 in. running area · 2.75 HP brushless · 0.6–6.2 mph · 10% incline · 350 lb max user · 12 HIIT programs · remote with mute",
    pros: [
      "A 350 lb weight limit for about $142, per the listing",
      "A 10% incline that adjusts in a second, without screws, per the listing",
      "Speeds to 6.2 mph, faster than the flat budget pads",
      "A remote with one-click mute for the beeps, and 12 HIIT programs",
    ],
    cons: [
      "The listing names no app or deck buttons as a backup to the remote",
      "Program selection is explained in the manual, not on the remote",
      "The 40 dB noise claim is the maker’s; we could not check it",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The budget pick for heavier users and for incline on a remote. Keep the remote somewhere fixed, as the listing itself advises.",
    description: "FOUSAE describes this as its third-generation walking pad, and on paper it offers the most for the money on this page. The listing gives a 2.75 HP brushless motor, a 0.6 to 6.2 mph speed range, a 350 lb maximum user weight, a built-in 10% incline that it says adjusts in one second without tightening screws, a 40 by 16-inch running area with a seven-layer belt and ten shock-absorbing columns, a full-width LED display and twelve HIIT programs plus time, distance and calorie countdown modes. The remote has a one-click mute for the beeps, which matters if you walk during calls. The listing is also unusually frank about the remote’s importance: it advises keeping the remote within reach to avoid misplacing it, and points to the manual and product videos for mode and program selection. It names no app and no deck buttons, so treat the remote as the only control until the manual says otherwise. With a margin of about 50 lb, the 350 lb limit suits users up to about 300 lb. FOUSAE promises replies to support questions within 18 hours. Amazon showed it at $142.49 on 24 November 2026.",
  },
  {
    id: "cybergoing-t10-walking-pad",
    name: "Cybergoing T10 Walking Pad",
