        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best walking pad with a remote?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, the WELLFIT 12% auto-incline walking pad, about $285 on Amazon on 24 November 2026. Its listing names six console buttons, voice commands and an app as well as the remote, so losing the remote does not stop a walk. On a budget, UREVO’s Smart Walking Pad offers a remote and app control for about $133.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a walking pad without the remote?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if it has another control. Of the six pads here, four listings name an app that changes speed and one (WELLFIT) names buttons on the machine. Goplus and FOUSAE name only the remote, so check the manual for deck buttons. A pad with no app and no deck keys cannot be started or sped up without its remote.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What battery does a walking pad remote take?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually a coin cell such as a CR2025 or two AAA batteries, judging by replacement remotes on Amazon. None of the six listings here states the battery, so open the cover when the pad arrives, note the size and keep a spare. A flat battery is the most common reason a remote stops working.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I buy a replacement remote for my walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually, yes. The maker’s own remote is the safest match; third-party replacements on Amazon cost about $10 to $25 but only work with the model codes they list. Find the code on the label under the deck or on the box, and match it before you order.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Why is my walking pad remote not working?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Check the battery first, then re-pair the remote using the routine in the manual, which usually means holding a button within a few seconds of switching the pad on. If the remote is infrared, make sure nothing blocks the pad’s receiver. If the display is dark or shows an error, the fault is the pad, not the remote.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad remote infrared or Bluetooth?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It varies, and most listings do not say. Infrared remotes are common on budget pads and generally need a clear path to the receiver; radio remotes are paired and work from a pocket. Bluetooth on these pads is usually the app connection. Ask the seller if the remote type matters to you.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

